# 20. Understanding belongsToMany()

This is the important relationship:

```php
public function projects(): BelongsToMany
{
    return $this->belongsToMany(Project::class)
                ->withPivot('role')
                ->withTimestamps();
}
```

It means:

```text
Employee
    ↓
employee_project
    ↓
Project
```

One employee can have many projects.

One project can have many employees.

Therefore:

```text
Many-to-Many
```

---

# 21. How Does Laravel Know About employee_project?

You may wonder:

"We never wrote:

```php
employee_project
```

inside `belongsToMany()`.

So how does Laravel know?"

Because Laravel follows conventions.

When you write:

```php
return $this->belongsToMany(Project::class);
```

Laravel understands:

```text
Current model = Employee
Related model = Project
```

Therefore Laravel expects:

```text
employee_project
```

as the pivot table.

It also expects:

```text
employee_id
project_id
```

as the pivot foreign keys.

So:

```php
$this->belongsToMany(Project::class);
```

is enough when you follow Laravel's naming conventions.

---

# 22. What Does withPivot('role') Do?

Our pivot table contains:

```text
employee_id
project_id
role
created_at
updated_at
```

By default, Eloquent knows about the relationship IDs.

But we also want to access:

```text
role
```

So we write:

```php
->withPivot('role')
```

Complete:

```php
public function projects(): BelongsToMany
{
    return $this->belongsToMany(Project::class)
                ->withPivot('role')
                ->withTimestamps();
}
```

Now we can do:

```php
$project->pivot->role;
```

---

# 23. What Does withTimestamps() Do?

Our pivot table has:

```text
created_at
updated_at
```

because we wrote:

```php
$table->timestamps();
```

To tell Laravel to maintain those pivot timestamps, we use:

```php
->withTimestamps();
```

Example:

```php
public function projects(): BelongsToMany
{
    return $this->belongsToMany(Project::class)
                ->withPivot('role')
                ->withTimestamps();
}
```

