# 30. sync()

`sync()` is used when you want the pivot relationships to match a specific list.

Example:

```php
$john->projects()->sync([
    $project1->id => [
        'role' => 'developer',
    ],

    $project2->id => [
        'role' => 'tester',
    ],
]);
```

This tells Laravel:

"John should have these projects and these pivot values."

Important:

`sync()` can remove relationships that are not included.

Suppose John currently has:

```text
Project 1
Project 2
Project 3
```

Then:

```php
$john->projects()->sync([
    $project1->id => [
        'role' => 'developer',
    ],
]);
```

After sync, John will have:

```text
Project 1
```

Project 2 and Project 3 relationships may be removed from the pivot table.

The actual projects are NOT deleted.

---

# 31. syncWithoutDetaching()

Sometimes you want to add or update relationships without removing existing ones.

Use:

```php
$john->projects()->syncWithoutDetaching([
    $project3->id => [
        'role' => 'developer',
    ],
]);
```

If John already has:

```text
Project 1
Project 2
```

then after this:

```text
Project 1
Project 2
Project 3
```

The existing relationships remain.

