# 42. with() — Eager Loading

Suppose we want all employees and their departments.

Without eager loading:

```php
$employees = Employee::all();

foreach ($employees as $employee) {
    echo $employee->department->name;
}
```

This can cause many database queries.

Instead:

```php
$employees = Employee::with('department')->get();
```

Now Laravel loads the employees and their departments efficiently.

---

# 43. with() Uses Model Relationships

When we write:

```php
Employee::with('department')->get();
```

Laravel looks at this method:

```php
public function department(): BelongsTo
{
    return $this->belongsTo(Department::class);
}
```

The string:

```text
department
```

refers to the relationship method:

```php
department()
```

Similarly:

```php
Employee::with('projects')->get();
```

uses:

```php
public function projects(): BelongsToMany
```

---

# 44. Eager Loading Projects

Use:

```php
$employees = Employee::with('projects')->get();
```

Then:

```php
foreach ($employees as $employee) {
    echo $employee->name;

    foreach ($employee->projects as $project) {
        echo $project->name;
    }
}
```

---

# 45. Eager Loading Multiple Relationships

You can load multiple relationships:

```php
$employees = Employee::with([
    'department',
    'projects',
])->get();
```

Now Laravel loads:

```text
Employee
 ├── Department
 └── Projects
```

---

# 46. Nested Eager Loading

Suppose:

```text
Department
    ↓
Employees
    ↓
Projects
```

You can use:

```php
$departments = Department::with('employees.projects')->get();
```

This means:

```text
Load departments
    ↓
load employees
    ↓
load each employee's projects
```

