# 51. Local Scope: active()

Our Employee model contains:

```php
public function scopeActive(Builder $query): void
{
    $query->where('is_active', true);
}
```

This creates a local scope called:

```text
active
```

We can use:

```php
Employee::active()->get();
```

This is equivalent to:

```php
Employee::where('is_active', true)->get();
```

---

# 52. Why Use a Scope?

Without scope:

```php
Employee::where('is_active', true)->get();
```

You might write the same condition in many places.

With scope:

```php
Employee::active()->get();
```

Your code becomes easier to read and reuse.

---

# 53. Combining Scope With Other Conditions

You can do:

```php
$employees = Employee::active()
    ->where('salary', '>', 50000)
    ->get();
```

This means:

```text
is_active = true
AND salary > 50000
```

---

# 54. Combining Scope With Eager Loading

You can also do:

```php
$employees = Employee::active()
    ->with('projects')
    ->get();
```

Now:

```text
Only active employees
        ↓
Their projects are eager loaded
```

